From f8136e1b9f3bb1249132d96dd9e31ea1c166f9c8 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Boo <boo@Apple-M4-Pro.local>
Date: Thu, 17 Sep 2026 02:59:14 +0700
Subject: [PATCH] Migrate ClickHouse from destroyed DO server to UpCloud
 (213.163.193.79)

The original ClickHouse host (167.172.71.234, DigitalOcean, no
backup/snapshot ever existed) was destroyed. Stood up a replacement on
UpCloud (rainbow-db) with a reconstructed schema (never had SSH access
to the original box in any session -- scripts/clickhouse_schema_reconstructed.sql
documents exactly what's high vs low confidence) and, this time, a real
password rather than an open default user.

Fixed several bugs surfaced only by the migration:
- get_clickhouse_client() in main.py hardcoded password="" instead of
  reading CLICKHOUSE_PASSWORD -- worked by accident against the old
  passwordless server, broke immediately against the new one.
- kafka_consumer.py's get_client() never passed user/password/database
  at all, same root cause.
- app/detail_user_activate.py, app/summary_hostname_cookie.py,
  app/test_db.py all hardcoded the ClickHouse IP directly instead of
  reading config -- switched to config.py/env everywhere.
- tracking-go's ClickHouse host/pass were compiled-in defaults because
  tracking.service had no EnvironmentFile; added one and dropped the
  hardcoded defaults so future config changes don't need a rebuild.
- Re-synced app/main.py from the live server: it had drifted from this
  repo (someone added /realtime, /today-stats, /weekly-summary directly
  on the box, which is what Admin Dashboard's 10-min/weekly Telegram
  report cron depends on) without ever being committed here.

Historical data from before Redpanda started buffering (~2026-09-07) is
gone for good. Everything produced since then is being recovered as
kafka-consumer catches up the Redpanda backlog against the new host.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Sonnet 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
